On Friday, June 7, 2013 12:49:59 AM UTC-4, Andria Dawson wrote:
> ####
>   for (k in 1:W) {
>     alpha[k] ~ multi_normal(zeros, C_star[k]);
>     g[k] <- mu[k]*ones + H[k]*alpha[k];
>   }
> ####

Also, if you are going to go to the expense of creating an array of C_star_inv matrices, then you can do

alpha[k] ~ multi_normal_prec(zeros, C_star_inv[k]);

to utilize the multivariate normal parameterized in terms of the precision matrix. The multi_normal() function essentially entails finding C_start_inv again (not literally, but it is still solving a matrix equation).

Ben

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Fri Jun  7 17:51:32 2013
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Fri, 07 Jun 2013 17:51:32 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] 'Matt trick' for logistic-binomial model
Message-ID: <L6pTF1VXgrA.mCrgY46QL8gJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/L6pTF1VXgrA/m/mCrgY46QL8gJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A serious problem with your model is that you declare
sigma2 as having support (0,infinity)

   real<lower=0> sigma2;

and then you use it with bounded support:

   sigma2 ~ uniform(0,100);

What you need to do is declare sigma2 with support
that matches the model.

   real<lower=0,upper=100> sigma2;

The re-encoding in model2 forgot the additive term for
the mean.  Instead of

transformed parameters {
   real u[G];
   for (g in 1:G)
     u[g] <- e_u[g] * sqrt(sigma2);
}

you need the assignment to include the mean u0:

   for (g in 1:G)
     u[g] <- u0 + e_u[g] * sqrt(sigma2);

but you really want to declare e_u as a vector:

    vector[G] e_u;

and then vectorize the assignment:

    u <- u0 + e_u * sqrt(sigma2);

In fact, for both speed and simplicity, I'd replace
prior for variance

   sigma2 ~ uniform(0,100);

with a uniform prior on standard deviation (changing the
constraint on its support to upper=10, of course!):

   sigma ~ uniform(0,10);

and then replace the vectorized assignment with

   u <- u0 + e_u * sigma;

- Bob

