Andrew,

Now that you have all the code in front of you, you'll see that I'm still using uniform priors for the variance components.  I want to be clear that I'm definitely not ignoring your advice on the half-Cauchy.  I'm still getting up to speed on how to implement this, so I decided to set it aside for the moment.  My thought was that the uniform prior would still be preferable to, say, an inverse-gamma.  I just ran across the half-Cauchy code on p.430 of Gelman and Hill (as well as the nice discussion of multiplicative redundant parameters on p.424-425), so I don't see any problem working this in very soon.

My understanding is that the half-Cauchy is even easier in Stan, in that you can safely ignore the half part of things, recognizing, of course, that calling the Cauchy distribution directly might not be the most computationally efficient approach.

This is obviously a separate issue from the speed and monitor concerns.  I should probably stop hijacking my own thread...

Adam

