Hi (again) Stan team,

Thanks again for the reply!

I am still trying to figure out what is going on with a positive definiteness problem for a covariance matrix. I get a non-fatal error every once in a while, and would like to track down exactly why I get this. Bob mentioned that we need strict positive definiteness. The error I am referring to is:

=====
Informational Message: The parameter state is about to be Metropolis rejected due to the following underlying, non-fatal (really) issue (and please ignore that what comes next might say 'error'): Error in function stan::prob::multi_normal_log(d): Covariance matrix is not positive definite. Covariance matrix(0,0) is 1.2429596267677166e-15.
====

I added some print statements to my model block to figure out which parameter combinations are throwing this error. Using the parameter combinations that are printed out, I can construct my covariance matrix. For the combinations that give me the error, I  find that my constructed matrix is positive definite (symmetric, full-rank, all eigenvalues greater than 0). My (0,0) entry of the matrix matches the number printed in the error, so I'm pretty sure I've got the right parameter set... (Granted the printed output has a different number of significant digits than Stan uses, so my computations will differ slightly from those Stan does.)

I attached some code, data, and a text file of the printed output I get. I ran this using Rstan with my seed set to 11.

Model is a Gaussian Process model linked to multinomial count data. Covariance matrix is negative exponential, with three parameters that are used to construct it (eta, rho, sigma_sq).

Any thoughts as to what is going on? I'm wondering if I am just missing something obvious here ...

In case this helps:
> sessionInfo()
R version 2.15.2 (2012-10-26)
Platform: x86_64-pc-linux-gnu (64-bit)

Thanks so much for all your help!
Andria

