>     compile a 'similar' input file (bivariate.R, added below) with the console displaying:
>
>
> If the parser (stanc) is compiled with optimization and you use C++11, then there will be a parser failure along the
> lines of what you found. There is some debate about whether this behavior is a bug on boost's part. Command-line Stan
> has O_STANC distinct from O so that you can compile stanc without optimization and the rest of stan with optimization.
> But rstan uses one optimization level for everything.
>
> Maybe rstan 2.0 will make this distinction. But for now, if you want to use C++-11, you are pretty much stuck with
> command-line Stan. However, Stan doesn't use any C++-11 features, so I doubt it would have a noticeable effect on
> performance. We've been trying to get compliant with the C++11 standard for a while but mostly to make sure that we
> don't have any undefined behavior.
>
> Ben
