From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Wed Jun 26 16:28:18 2013
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Wed, 26 Jun 2013 16:28:18 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] "real a[2]" vs "vector[2] a" ?
Message-ID: <4PgOF38Mnwk.qPnzRQvM0Q0J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <4PgOF38Mnwk.WuH7wz-agCEJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <4PgOF38Mnwk.WuH7wz-agCEJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/4PgOF38Mnwk/m/qPnzRQvM0Q0J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

They have slightly different ops defined on them, but
we'll be unifying those in the future.

In general, you should prefer vector unless there's an
operation that only works on arrays.  The reason is that
the vector/matrix/linear-algebra ops are only defined on
vectors and all the multivariate distributions are defined
in terms of vector/matrix parameters.

Also note that arrays of row vectors behave very similarly
to matrices:

    matrix[M,N] x;

vs.

    row_vector[N] x[M];

The latter array-based version has faster row access if you
use x[m] (which is row m of the matrix x).

- Bob

